{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a soft pink snow drifting over temple roofs. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet reverence of Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion, shimmering above its mirror pond. I sat on a wooden bench, sipping matcha as an elderly woman swept leaves with deliberate, meditative strokes. Time slowed here.\n\nTokyo was a shock of light and motion. Shibuya’s scramble crossing pulsed like a living organism; I got swept up in it, laughing as strangers moved in perfect, unspoken rhythm. In a tiny alley in Shinjuku, I found a 70-year-old ramen master who didn’t speak English but smiled as he slid a steaming bowl toward me—perfectly chewy noodles, rich broth, a single slice of chashu that melted like butter. I ate in silence, grateful.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was hushed, the Atomic Bomb Dome standing as a silent witness. I folded a paper crane at the Children’s Peace Monument, my fingers clumsy but my heart clear.\n\nIn Kanazawa, I wandered Kenrokuen Garden, the most perfect landscape I’ve ever seen—snow-laden pines, stone lanterns, a pond like liquid jade. A geisha passed me, her silk kimono rustling, eyes unreadable, elegant.\n\nI bought a single yuzu-scented candle in Nara and lit it that night in my ryokan. As the scent curled into the air, I thought: Japan doesn’t shout. It whispers—and if you listen, it changes you.\n\nI left with no souvenirs but a quiet mind, a full heart, and the taste of matcha still on my tongue. Japan didn’t give me memories. It gave me stillness.\n\n— Word count: 298", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logpro